Origin
------

Silo began on 11 April 2026 as a fork of Seafile Server
(https://github.com/haiwen/seafile-server), originally developed by
Seafile Ltd. and licensed under the AGPL.

Nothing of that codebase remains in a form its authors would recognise.
The C daemon, the Python web layer, the sync protocol and the on-disk
object format have all been replaced. Silo is a single Go binary speaking
its own HTTP API over its own store format, and no upstream client can
talk to it.

That claim is measured rather than asserted, and the measurement is
one anybody can repeat. Take the fork point, commit 9cf6605 of the
upstream tree; for every path that existed there and still exists here,
strip the whitespace from both versions and count the distinct
non-trivial lines that appear in both. As of September 2026 that is
eighty-six lines across seven files, and each one is a line with a
single correct spelling -- `package objstore`, `import (`,
`if err != nil {` -- dictated by Go or by a library's API.

Read that number as a tripwire rather than as proof. It over-counts,
because `if err != nil {` is shared by every Go program ever written and
says nothing about descent. It under-counts more seriously, because a
file that was renamed, or reformatted, or whose logic was carried across
in different words does not appear in it at all. It is evidence of what
plainly has not been cleared, never evidence that something was.

This notice is a courtesy, not an obligation. Silo is under the AGPL
because that is the licence it was forked under and the licence its
author chose to keep -- not because upstream code survives to require it.


What was removed from the inherited licence
-------------------------------------------

Seafile's LICENSE file carried an additional permission, granted in
Seafile Ltd.'s name, to link the covered work with OpenSSL. Silo cannot
make a grant on Seafile Ltd.'s behalf, and has no use for one: it is pure
Go, links no OpenSSL, and reaches TLS through crypto/tls. Section 7 of
the AGPL expressly allows an additional permission to be removed from a
copy when that copy is conveyed, so it has been. Anyone who wants that
permission for upstream's code can take it from upstream's copy, where it
still stands.

The same file placed a python directory under Apache License 2.0. Silo
has no python directory.

LICENSE.txt is now the Free Software Foundation's text, unmodified, under
a statement of Silo's own copyright.


